WELL REASONED REFACTORS:
- seperate staging allocation from the command pool.
  may either be another independent submodule, which is used by the cmd
  or be another header inside of the cmd module. (i think if we can split it, it should be worth it).
  Would later be easy to refactor if we see that a single staging buffer of a pool grows to much and a
  single staging buffer across all command pools actually performs better.
  
  staging component, would only depend on memory and buf

  Could possibly also be something that could later reuse for scratch or other applications like 
  descriptor heap uploads paths or something like that.

  thread-safe scratch could actually be quite nice, the downsides seem reasonable.

  The previously nice part is that because pools are already externally synchronized 
  we scratch allocation didn't need to be internally synchronized but doing so would probably not 
  lead to a lot of performance degration especially because block allocation is fairly rare.
  => Do this one it's reasonable next step in cleaning this codebase up and moving towards descriptor heaps.



