Milestones:

DescriptorHeap: 
  So we did now think about this a lot and the cleanest solution seems to be to have 
  one internal descriptor heap, which manages it's own memory, and may even grow. 
  This can be achieved either through ReBAR or through async transfers and timeline semaphores.
  The proposed interface would then just be 
  Device::create_buffer_descriptor({info}); this returns a immutable RAII handle 
  which can be used through some magical API as a push constant and be bound to a command buffer state.

  We can make the RAII mess a bit simpler, by also exposing ideas like a BufferDescriptorArrays or 
  ImageDescriptorArrays, which internally still just hold references to Descriptors, but adding them to a state 
  only decs one count and not thousands. We will have to see if the Descriptor handle allocation can be done lazily 
  or something like that. DescriptorTables would be a similar idea, but probably less required.

  The ReBAR or async transfer implementation should be completely hidden to the user, which with async transfer we should be 
  able to do, but object ownership transfer still has to be figured out. Maintainance9 seems like it removes explicit 
  queue ownership transfer for buffer, which would fit nicely here. 



